const mahaviraSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence','A teacher recoverable through competing memories',[
    'Mahāvīra, also called Vardhamāna, is the earliest Jain teacher whose historical existence is supported both inside and outside Jain traditions. Early Buddhist literature remembers a Nigaṇṭha Nātaputta leading an established ascetic community, a name conventionally connected with Mahāvīra. That external attestation matters, but it is not a neutral biography: Buddhist texts present rivals for Buddhist purposes. Jain scriptures and later lives preserve much richer narratives, yet these too arrange events around the exemplary career of a Jina. A responsible profile therefore begins with a historical ascetic teacher in the eastern Gangetic region and keeps each narrative’s genre visible.',
    'The familiar Śvetāmbara dates 599–527 BCE and the Digambara dates 582–510 BCE are traditional chronologies. Modern reconstructions often place Mahāvīra later, partly because the Buddha’s chronology has itself shifted, but no alternative pair of exact years is secure enough to replace tradition with documentary precision. This profile displays the traditional range as tradition, uses the broad sixth-to-fifth-century BCE setting for history, and avoids pretending that a numeric birth and death anchor measures certainty. He was probably a contemporary or near-contemporary of the Buddha and other renunciant teachers; that relation is safer than an exact synchronized timeline.',
  ],['jainism','indian-philosophy'],['buddha'],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra']),
  section('lineage','The twenty-fourth Jina, not a simple founder',[
    'Jain traditions identify Mahāvīra as the twenty-fourth Tīrthaṃkara, or ford-maker, of the present descending cosmic half-cycle. A Jina is a victor over attachment and karma who realizes unobstructed knowledge and reopens a path for others. Within that account Jain teaching is neither invented by Mahāvīra nor created once in linear history; Jinas articulate an eternal structure of reality and liberation when it has become obscured. Calling him “founder of Jainism” therefore misstates the tradition’s self-understanding and erases the communities and disciplines said to precede him.',
    'Historical analysis does not have to affirm the entire cosmic lineage in order to report it accurately. The first twenty-two Tīrthaṃkaras belong principally to sacred history rather than recoverable chronology. Pārśvanātha, the twenty-third, has a stronger claim to represent an earlier ascetic tradition, though his dates and biography also remain uncertain. Śvetāmbara accounts often say that Pārśva taught four restraints and Mahāvīra made celibacy explicit as a fifth; Digambara accounts do not accept that doctrinal difference in the same way. The cautious historical claim is reform and reorganization of an earlier nirgrantha milieu, not invention from nothing.',
  ],['jainism'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra']),
  section('lives','Sacred biography and sectarian difference',[
    'Later Jain lives organize Mahāvīra’s career through auspicious conception and birth, princely household life, renunciation, severe austerity, omniscience, teaching, community formation, and final liberation. These are not disposable embellishments: they show what Jain communities understand a Jina to be and supply themes for ritual, festival, pilgrimage, manuscript painting, and ethical imagination. Yet the pattern is hagiographical rather than a diary. Details such as exact places, dreams, royal relationships, named episodes, and the duration of stages must be attributed to particular textual and sectarian traditions rather than narrated as uncontested eyewitness fact.',
    'Śvetāmbara sources tell of an embryo transferred from the Brahmin Devānandā to the Kṣatriya Triśalā and generally portray Vardhamāna as marrying and having a daughter before renunciation. Digambara tradition rejects the embryo-transfer episode and ordinarily denies the marriage. The two traditions also differ about clothing, food after omniscience, canonical survival, and implications of embodiment and gender for liberation. These disagreements cannot be solved by splicing favored details into one smooth life. They reveal how biography became a vehicle for disputes about perfect renunciation, authority, community memory, and who can attain the highest state.',
  ],['jainism'],[],['Kalpa Sūtra','Ācārāṅga Sūtra']),
  section('asceticism','Renunciation as transformation of action and attention',[
    'The Ācārāṅga depicts Mahāvīra’s ascetic course through exposure, fasting, silence, restraint, insults, injuries, careful movement, and radical nonretaliation. Even where a scene is not independently recoverable, the portrait explains the discipline transmitted in his name. Asceticism is not merely toughness or punishment of the body. It aims to stop fresh karmic influx, wear away accumulated karma, weaken passions, and disentangle the conscious self from material bondage. Conduct and knowledge are therefore inseparable: anger, carelessness, possession, and appetite both harm living beings and cloud the agent’s capacity to perceive them rightly.',
    'Modern admiration for resilience can distort this project if it turns Mahāvīra into a productivity coach. His disciplines belong to a cosmology of rebirth and liberation, and the mendicant ideal is intentionally more demanding than ordinary social life. Jain communities nevertheless developed graded lay vows, almsgiving, confession, fasting, worship, and support for mendicants rather than requiring every person to imitate the most extreme austerities immediately. The gap between great vows and limited vows is not hypocrisy; it is an institutional recognition that household responsibilities and complete renunciation occupy different stations on the path.',
  ],['jainism','ethics'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('ahimsa','Nonviolence begins with a densely living world',[
    'Ahiṃsā, noninjury, is central because Jain ontology treats living selves as present far beyond the human and animal cases most moral theories consider. Plants and elemental bodies enter detailed classifications of life, while beings possess different sensory capacities and vulnerabilities. Harm may occur through body, speech, or mind, by doing, ordering, consenting, or careless participation. The vow consequently trains perception before it supplies a slogan: one must learn to notice lives, dependencies, tools, movements, foods, occupations, and passions that ordinary habit renders invisible. Restraint is an epistemic education in the consequences of action.',
    'Absolute harmlessness is impossible for an embodied householder, and even mendicants move, breathe, and consume. Jain ethics responds through vigilance, minimization, intention, confession, and differentiated vows, not through the fantasy of material innocence. This framework supports vegetarianism and extensive care in food and work, yet “Jainism means veganism” is historically too simple: actual practices vary by time, place, sect, medicine, livelihood, and debates about unseen life. The demanding insight is that unavoidable harm remains morally significant. One should reduce and understand it without converting impossibility into permission or personal purity into indifference toward structural violence.',
  ],['jainism','ethics'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('vows','Five restraints and the passions beneath action',[
    'The five great vows are nonviolence, truthfulness, not taking what is not given, celibacy, and nonpossession. They form a connected discipline rather than five isolated prohibitions. Falsehood can recruit others into harm; taking expresses disregard for another’s agency; sexual attachment and possession intensify craving, dependence, concealment, and fear. Later Jain analysis further attends to anger, pride, deceit, and greed as passions that bind karma. Ethical evaluation thus reaches below visible outcomes to the habits and affects organizing conduct, while still preserving the concrete priority of not injuring living beings.',
    'Truthfulness is limited by nonviolence: speaking a fact in a way that predictably exposes someone to harm cannot be defended merely as candor. Nonpossession also reaches beyond counting objects. A person can be attached to status, relationships, opinions, or the self-image of renunciation. Yet the tradition does not collapse all social duties into inward intention. Rules about movement, food, tools, commerce, and care make discipline public and accountable. Mahāvīra’s transmitted ethical importance lies in this union of exacting outward restraint with an equally exacting diagnosis of the passions that make harm appear natural or necessary.',
  ],['jainism','ethics'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('self-karma','A conscious self bound by material karma',[
    'In Jain philosophy the jīva is a living, conscious self, not a temporary stream with no enduring subject and not an inactive witness untouched by agency. Its capacities for knowledge and perception can become obscured, contracted, and entangled through karma. Karma is not merely moral scorekeeping or figurative consequence; systematic Jain accounts describe subtle matter that flows toward and binds the self under conditions shaped by activity and passion. Liberation requires stopping influx and shedding what has accumulated. This makes ethics, metaphysics, psychology, and soteriology parts of one explanatory project.',
    'The framework differs both from Buddhist no-self arguments and from Sāṃkhya accounts that separate passive consciousness from active material nature. Jain authors defend an agent who genuinely acts, bears consequences, and can transform. They also maintain a plurality of selves rather than one universal consciousness. A beginner may compare dirt obscuring a luminous surface, but the analogy has limits: karmic classifications are elaborate causal theories, not just metaphors for guilt. The crucial point for Mahāvīra’s path is practical—freedom requires changing the activities and attachments through which the self participates in its own bondage.',
  ],['jainism','metaphysics','indian-philosophy'],['buddha'],['Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('knowledge','Knowledge, perspective, and later systematization',[
    'Mahāvīra is central to Jain claims about omniscient teaching, but the mature doctrines now associated with Jain epistemology were articulated across centuries. Anekāntavāda, the many-sidedness of reality, naya theories of partial standpoints, and forms of conditional predication became sophisticated responses to rival accounts of substance, change, and knowledge. They should not be placed as fully formed technical treatises in the mouth of the historical Mahāvīra. Their authority is genealogically tied to the Jina’s teaching, while their surviving formulations belong to later canonical, commentarial, and debate settings.',
    'Many-sidedness does not say that every opinion is equally true. Jain thinkers are realists: objects have complex features independently of what a speaker happens to prefer, and a claim succeeds by specifying a relevant standpoint, time, place, mode, and respect. An omniscient knower would apprehend the whole; ordinary speakers capture limited aspects and can mistake limitation for completeness. The ethical parallel is important but should not become a vague tolerance slogan. Intellectual non-one-sidedness demands disciplined qualification, engagement with opponents, and reasons for selecting a perspective. It restrains dogmatism without abolishing contradiction, evidence, or error.',
  ],['jainism','epistemology','logic'],[],['Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('community','Teaching through a fourfold community',[
    'Jain tradition credits Mahāvīra with organizing a fourfold community of monks, nuns, laymen, and laywomen. Exact numbers and named lineages in later texts are not secure demographic records, but the structure matters. Liberation teaching survives through reciprocal, unequal roles: mendicants model and transmit intensive discipline, while householders provide food, shelter, protection, patronage, and family continuity. Women appear prominently in the remembered community even as later Śvetāmbara and Digambara traditions disagree about clothing, embodiment, mendicant perfection, and whether a woman can attain liberation without male rebirth.',
    'The teaching community also complicates the image of a solitary hero. Mahāvīra’s authority is presented as extraordinary knowledge, yet what later Jains receive depends on disciples, memorization, councils, teachers, commentaries, donors, temples, storytellers, and sectarian institutions. Śvetāmbaras preserve an Āgama canon they connect with oral transmission from his disciples; Digambaras hold that the original canon was lost and authorize other textual lineages. Neither claim licenses the historian to treat a surviving scripture as an unfiltered transcript. Transmission is itself part of the subject, not noise to be removed from a pristine origin. The traditional gaṇadharas, especially Indrabhūti Gautama and Sudharman, matter here as remembered mediators. Historical caution about their biographies should not make the labor, vulnerability, and institutional creativity of succession disappear.',
  ],['jainism'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('death-reception','Liberation, memory, and political afterlives',[
    'Tradition locates Mahāvīra’s final liberation at Pāvā and connects it with the festival later celebrated as Dīvālī. A liberated siddha has shed all karma and rises to the summit of the cosmos, no longer acting in the world or answering prayer as a creator deity. Devotion to Mahāvīra therefore does not require that he intervene supernaturally. Images, hymns, pilgrimage, offerings, and narrative can orient devotees toward the qualities of a perfected being and sustain the community that practices his path. The distinction between exemplary worship and creator-god dependence is essential to understanding Jain devotion.',
    'Mahāvīra’s reception extends from monastic law and philosophical debate to art, commerce, reform, nationalism, environmental ethics, and global nonviolence. Gandhi learned from Jain interlocutors and a wider Indian history of ahiṃsā, but Jain nonviolence should not be reduced to a preface to Gandhi. Nor should modern ecological comparison erase the path’s ascetic and karmic aims. The most responsible contemporary use asks what disciplined attention to vulnerable life can contribute while acknowledging disagreements about caste, gender, wealth, ritual, mendicancy, and the institutional conditions under which minimizing harm becomes possible.',
  ],['jainism','ethics'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra']),
  section('reading','A reading path that keeps traditions distinct',[
    'Begin with the Ācārāṅga’s first book in a named translation, reading its descriptions of restraint and Mahāvīra’s austerities as a Śvetāmbara canonical witness rather than stenography. Pair it immediately with a historical overview by Dundas or Jaini. Mark which statements are early ascetic teaching, later biography, modern reconstruction, or the translator’s vocabulary. Then use the Tattvārtha Sūtra to see how a later systematic text relates right view, knowledge, conduct, living beings, karma, vows, and liberation. This sequence moves from remembered practice to organized doctrine without pretending they are contemporary documents.',
    'Next compare Śvetāmbara and Digambara accounts of marriage, clothing, food, scripture, women’s liberation, and the earlier Pārśva lineage. The point is not to crown one modernized biography but to learn how authority is argued through bodies, texts, and succession. Finish with specialist discussions of ahiṃsā and many-sidedness, testing popular slogans against technical contexts. Whenever an English quotation matters, name its translator and standard division. Whenever Mahāvīra is credited with a mature philosophical doctrine, ask whether the evidence belongs to an early source, a later systematizer, a commentator, or a contemporary interpreter.',
  ],['jainism','indian-philosophy'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
];

const jainismSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation','A tradition without a single historical beginning',[
    'Jainism is a South Asian religious and philosophical tradition organized around Jinas, awakened victors who reveal a ford across rebirth. Jains understand that teaching as eternal: cosmic time moves through vast rising and declining cycles, and twenty-four Tīrthaṃkaras appear in each relevant half-cycle. Mahāvīra is the twenty-fourth of the present cycle, not the inventor of truth. Historical inquiry uses a different timescale. It can identify an ancient nirgrantha renunciant movement, Mahāvīra’s early community, and a plausible earlier Pārśva tradition without turning the whole sacred lineage into recoverable chronology or dismissing its religious meaning.',
    'The tradition is neither one doctrine frozen in the sixth century BCE nor merely an ethic of being nice to animals. It includes mendicant lineages, lay communities, philosophies of self and matter, cosmologies, ritual and devotional practices, narrative literatures, temples, pilgrimage, mercantile and agricultural histories, art, law, languages, reform movements, and diasporas. “Jaina philosophy” names arguments developed within these worlds, often in debate with Buddhist, Nyāya, Mīmāṃsā, Sāṃkhya, Vedānta, and other thinkers. An entity-complete page must keep philosophical analysis connected to the disciplines and institutions that give it purpose.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','ethics'],['mahavira'],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('history','From early renunciants to durable communities',[
    'Mahāvīra belongs to the era of eastern Gangetic urbanization, new states, itinerant teachers, sacrifice debates, and renunciant experiments. Buddhist texts attest a Nigaṇṭha community and its leader, while Jain traditions remember predecessors and a fourfold order. Exact dependency claims are risky: Jainism should not be made a Buddhist offshoot, a simple reaction to a uniform “Brahmanism,” or the unchanged survival of a prehistoric religion. Its early history emerges from shared vocabularies and competitive practices among several ascetic cultures, with distinctive commitments becoming clearer through transmission and debate.',
    'After Mahāvīra, communities spread across regions and languages under changing political patronage. Councils and lineages are remembered as responses to famine, migration, memory loss, and disagreement, but traditional chronologies cannot always be confirmed. By the early centuries CE, boundaries between traditions called Śvetāmbara and Digambara had hardened around clothing, scripture, mendicant conduct, biography, and liberation. Later temple cultures, courtly intellectuals, merchants, poets, nuns, monks, and lay teachers made Jainism prominent in western, central, and southern India. No single migration story explains this complex institutional geography.',
  ],['indian-philosophy'],['mahavira'],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra']),
  section('scriptures','Canons, losses, and commentarial authority',[
    'Śvetāmbara traditions preserve an Āgama canon in Ardhamāgadhī and related Prakrits, held to transmit Mahāvīra’s teaching through disciples and later redaction. Digambaras maintain that the original canon was eventually lost and rely on distinct early doctrinal and disciplinary works. These are not minor editions of one uncontested Bible. Lists, textual boundaries, languages, dates, and authority differ within as well as across communities. The surviving Ācārāṅga is invaluable for early ascetic ideals, yet its layers and Śvetāmbara transmission prevent its use as a literal transcript of Mahāvīra.',
    'The Sanskrit Tattvārtha Sūtra, attributed as Umāsvāti or Umāsvāmi, holds unusual authority across the two major traditions, though recensions and commentaries differ. Its chapters coordinate right view, knowledge, living and nonliving realities, cosmology, karmic influx and bondage, stoppage, shedding, vows, and liberation. Later authors such as Kundakunda, Samantabhadra, Akalaṅka, Haribhadra, Hemacandra, Yaśovijaya, and many others develop divergent idioms. A bibliography that jumps from Mahāvīra to a mature doctrine without naming its textual author launders centuries of intellectual work into a founder figure.',
  ],['jainism','indian-philosophy'],['mahavira'],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('reality','Living selves in an uncreated cosmos',[
    'Jain accounts distinguish jīva, living conscious self, from ajīva, nonliving reality. Systematic taxonomies describe substances including selves, matter, the media or conditions of motion and rest, space, and time. The universe is beginningless and uncreated; gods exist within it but are embodied and karmically conditioned rather than creators standing outside its order. Liberated selves also do not govern the world. Calling Jainism simply “atheist” can therefore mislead: it rejects a creator and includes divine beings, perfected exemplars, worship, cosmological realms, and religious authority in a configuration unlike modern secular naturalism.',
    'Each living self has consciousness as an essential capacity, though karmic obstruction restricts its manifestation. Souls inhabit bodies ranging across detailed classes of sensory life, including one-sensed beings associated with plants and elements. Pluralism is fundamental: liberation does not dissolve selves into one absolute. This ontology grounds responsibility because an agent genuinely acts and experiences results. It also grounds nonviolence because the field of morally considerable life is extraordinarily dense. A stone, water body, plant, insect, animal, and human are not interchangeable, but everyday matter cannot be assumed lifeless without disciplined classification.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('karma','Karma as bondage, influx, stoppage, and shedding',[
    'Karma in Jain philosophy is subtle material bondage, not only the social proverb that deeds come back to their author. Activities of body, speech, and mind create influx, while passions such as anger, pride, deceit, and greed condition binding. Elaborate karmic types explain effects on knowledge, perception, feeling, status, lifespan, embodiment, and capacity. The model gives moral psychology a material-cosmological structure: attachment shapes both what one does and how one can know. Good karma can improve rebirth yet remains bondage; final freedom requires release from auspicious as well as harmful accumulation.',
    'The path is often explained through a sequence: recognize bondage, stop new influx through restraint, and shed accumulated karma through ascetic discipline and equanimity. This framework prevents two common simplifications. First, suffering is not a warrant to blame victims casually; outsiders rarely know the karmic histories posited by the system, and compassion remains a duty. Second, ritual or donation cannot purchase liberation mechanically while passions continue unchanged. Community practices may support right orientation and mendicants, but the decisive transformation concerns the self’s activity, attachment, knowledge, and conduct across a long path.',
  ],['ethics','metaphysics'],[],['Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('path','Three jewels and graded discipline',[
    'The path is commonly coordinated as right view, right knowledge, and right conduct—the three jewels. They are mutually dependent rather than three tickets collected in any order. Knowledge without transformed conduct remains bound to passion; conduct without right orientation can become mechanical or proud; conviction without inquiry can harden into one-sidedness. Omniscience represents the full removal of knowledge-obscuring karma, while final liberation follows the exhaustion of all karma. The philosophical ideal is therefore simultaneously cognitive, ethical, and soteriological, not a choice between correct belief and good behavior.',
    'Mendicants undertake great vows; householders adopt limited vows and supplementary practices suited to family, work, property, and civic life. Both may engage confession, fasting, meditation, study, worship, pilgrimage, giving, dietary discipline, and care for living beings, but their obligations differ. Sallekhanā, a highly regulated fast at the approach of death, is understood within Jain traditions as dispassionate thinning of body and passions, not ordinary suicide; its practice and legal interpretation remain contested. Responsible explanation neither sensationalizes it nor removes the stringent conditions, risks, and modern debates surrounding agency.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('ahimsa','Nonviolence as vow, perception, and social problem',[
    'Ahiṃsā prohibits injury while cultivating awareness of how harm travels through intention, carelessness, speech, tools, food, commerce, and consent. The tradition’s minute disciplines—examining spaces, limiting movement, filtering water in some contexts, regulating meals, avoiding occupations tied to killing—make visible consequences that moral theory often abstracts away. Truthfulness, nonstealing, chastity, and nonpossession support this central vow by reducing deception, exploitation, craving, and fear. Nonviolence is not passive avoidance: it is the positive labor of reorganizing desire and material dependence around vulnerable life.',
    'Lay existence makes complete noninjury impossible, so Jain ethics works with degrees, vows, intention, and continual repair. That realism can support contemporary discussion of food systems, animal use, climate, business, and war, but application requires more than extracting a timeless principle. Historical Jain households participated in hierarchy, wealth, trade, patronage, and political power; communities debate caste, gender, environment, laboratory science, and economic responsibility. The tradition offers unusually rigorous tools for auditing harm while also facing the question every nonviolent ethic faces: how restraint by individuals relates to institutions that distribute injury collectively.',
  ],['ethics'],[],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('many-sidedness','Many-sided objects and disciplined assertion',[
    'Anekāntavāda holds that real things possess multiple aspects that no ordinary finite assertion captures exhaustively. Jain thinkers use substance and mode to explain how something can persist through change: a clay pot may continue in one respect while acquiring or losing states in another. Naya theory identifies partial standpoints, and syādvāda formulates qualified predication through the particle syāt, roughly “in some respect” or “conditionally.” The sevenfold scheme coordinates affirmation, negation, both in sequence, and inexpressibility under specified conditions rather than celebrating flat contradiction.',
    'These doctrines matured in debate and cannot all be assigned word-for-word to Mahāvīra. Nor are they relativism in which any claim becomes true if one adds “perhaps.” A standpoint must answer to an independently structured object, and incompatible claims may concern different times, modes, locations, or explanatory purposes. Omniscience remains the regulative contrast to partial ordinary cognition. The ethical affinity with nonviolence is real but indirect: restraining absolutist assertion can reduce intellectual aggression, yet a polite falsehood is still false. The method demands clarification and argument, not merely tolerance or compromise.',
  ],['epistemology','logic','metaphysics'],[],['Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('sects-gender','Śvetāmbara and Digambara plurality',[
    'Śvetāmbara, “white-clad,” and Digambara, “sky-clad,” name major formations with further internal lineages. The clothing contrast concerns male mendicant possession and perfect renunciation, not a dress preference. Differences also involve which scriptures survive, Mahāvīra’s biography, whether an omniscient being consumes food, images and ritual, and women’s liberation. Śvetāmbara traditions recognize clothed mendicancy and women’s capacity for liberation in a female birth; influential Digambara positions require male rebirth for final liberation and reserve nudity for fully qualified male mendicants. Actual histories contain more variety than this binary summary.',
    'Women have long formed substantial communities of renunciants and lay practitioners, yet numerical presence does not eliminate asymmetries of authority, mobility, education, or ritual status. The traditions also contain debates over the gender of Mallī, the nineteenth Tīrthaṃkara. These issues should not be hidden to protect a harmonious image or used to reduce Jainism to one modern verdict. They show metaphysics, clothing, embodiment, scripture, and institution interacting. Contemporary Jain women and reformers interpret inherited resources in diverse ways, and no single spokesperson exhausts either sect or global Jain practice.',
  ],['jainism','ethics'],['mahavira'],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('worship-modernity','Devotion, reform, and contemporary relevance',[
    'Jain worship can include images of Jinas, temples, hymns, offerings, circumambulation, festival, pilgrimage, and veneration of teachers. Because liberated Jinas do not intervene as creators, these practices are often explained as praise and self-orientation rather than petition for divine rescue. Yet lived devotion has emotional, communal, aesthetic, and material dimensions that a purely philosophical account misses. Image-rejecting Śvetāmbara lineages, temple-centered communities, Digambara practices, regional forms, and lay household rites differ. “Non-theistic” does not mean religion without devotion, sacred presence, or contested ritual authority.',
    'Modern Jains have connected ahiṃsā with animal protection, vegetarian and vegan advocacy, environmentalism, peace work, medicine, and business ethics; anuvrat movements reformulated limited vows for public life. Diaspora institutions translate practice for new legal, occupational, and dietary settings. Comparison with pluralism, virtue ethics, environmental ethics, or animal ethics can be illuminating if karma, liberation, mendicancy, and cosmology are not stripped away. The tradition challenges modern readers to treat consumption as morally charged while asking its own communities how wealth, caste, gender, and institutional power fit the discipline of nonpossession. Hospitals, animal shelters, schools, publishing projects, and digital teaching also show that “withdrawal from the world” never describes the whole community. These institutions can embody care while raising hard questions about triage, resources, professional expertise, and indirect injury. Jain ethical reflection therefore concerns both the renouncer’s vigilance and the lay institution’s continuing accountability.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
  section('reading','Reading across practice, doctrine, and disagreement',[
    'Begin with selected Ācārāṅga lectures on life and restraint, always naming Jacobi’s historical translation and its Śvetāmbara setting. Read Jaini or Dundas beside it to identify composition, transmission, community, and sectarian issues. Then work through the Tattvārtha Sūtra in Tatia’s translation: chapters 1 and 2 orient knowledge and living reality; chapters 6 through 10 trace influx, bondage, stoppage, shedding, and liberation. Keep a glossary for jīva, ajīva, āsrava, bandha, saṃvara, nirjarā, mokṣa, and ahiṃsā rather than assuming one English equivalent settles meaning.',
    'Next use Gorisse’s specialist overview to connect karma, self, omniscience, atomism, perspectives, and predication. Compare later authors instead of synthesizing them into one anonymous “Jain view,” and deliberately read both Śvetāmbara and Digambara accounts of canon, conduct, and gender. Finish by testing one contemporary application—animal agriculture, investment, climate, speech, or conflict—against graded vows and unavoidable harm. A serious reading path should make the tradition harder and more coherent at once: clearer in its concepts, more diverse in its history, and less available as a collection of decontextualized slogans. Add one practice-centered source or community account when studying lived religion, but do not use present testimony to erase older textual difference. Ask which bodies, occupations, and institutions make a proposed vow possible for some practitioners and difficult for others. Finally, revisit the application after reading a sectarian disagreement, because intellectual humility requires testing whether the category “Jain” has hidden a consequential difference among texts, communities, or disciplines.',
  ],['jainism','indian-philosophy','ethics'],['mahavira'],['Ācārāṅga Sūtra','Tattvārtha Sūtra']),
];

const kanadaSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence','A name attached to a layered school text',[
    'Kaṇāda is the traditional sage associated with the Vaiśeṣika Sūtra, but almost nothing independently biographical can be recovered. Names such as Ulūka and Kaṇabhakṣa appear in later traditions, sometimes explained through stories about gathering or eating particles. These narratives belong to reception and etymological imagination, not documentary life. Even the date of the sūtra collection is contested. Proposals range across several centuries before and around the beginning of the Common Era because scholars reason from language, doctrinal relations, and possible opponents rather than securely dated manuscripts or events.',
    'The safest Atlas identity is therefore “traditional author of the Vaiśeṣika Sūtra,” with a broad pre-Common-Era-to-early-Common-Era textual horizon and low biographical confidence. Numeric years in older summaries should not be read as a lifespan. The received work is also likely layered: terse aphorisms were transmitted, arranged, interpreted, and perhaps altered over time. A claim can be securely about the received sūtra tradition while remaining uncertain about whether one historical Kaṇāda formulated it, when each passage entered it, or what its earliest audience understood without commentary.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','metaphysics'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('sutra','How to read an aphoristic teaching instrument',[
    'A sūtra compresses a field of instruction into brief formulations suited to memorization and explanation by a teacher. The Vaiśeṣika Sūtra opens by announcing dharma and connects knowledge of reality’s distinctive principles with the highest good. It then enumerates substances, qualities, actions, generality, particularity, and inherence; analyzes motion, causation, cognition, conjunction, disjunction, and other problems; and preserves material from different argumentative settings. Reading an English list of propositions as a self-sufficient modern treatise misses the oral and commentarial practices that made these aphorisms intelligible.',
    'The widely available Sinha translation follows a non-critical text together with Śaṅkara Miśra’s much later Upaskāra. It is valuable for locating received aphorisms, not a transparent window into the earliest recension. Candrānanda’s commentary preserves another important textual witness, while Chinese evidence and quotations in later authors complicate reconstruction. For this reason, the page cites standard book and aphorism divisions when possible but uses specialist histories for doctrinal development. It never converts an explanation supplied by a medieval commentator into Kaṇāda’s securely documented personal position without qualification.',
  ],['indian-philosophy'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('categories','Padārtha as a map of what can be known and said',[
    'Vaiśeṣika is famous for categories, padārthas: substance, quality, action or motion, universal, particularity, and inherence in the classical sixfold list. Later Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika adds absence as a seventh category and systematizes definitions more sharply. “Category” is useful but imperfect, because padārtha can mean the referent or object of a word and links ontology with classification and language. The project asks what kinds of entity must be admitted to explain ordinary differentiation, persistence, change, predication, and causal connection. It is not merely an ancient filing cabinet for physical objects.',
    'The categories do different work. Substances bear qualities and actions; qualities such as color or number do not move independently; actions help produce conjunction and disjunction; universals explain repeatable classification; ultimate particularizers distinguish eternal entities of the same kind; inherence relates items taken to be inseparable, such as a quality and its bearer or a whole and its parts. Later authors refine and dispute every definition. A visitor should learn the explanatory problem behind each name, not memorize a list and attribute its final scholastic form unchanged to the earliest sūtras.',
  ],['metaphysics','logic','philosophy-of-language'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('substances','Nine substances and a plural realist world',[
    'Mature Vaiśeṣika recognizes earth, water, fire, air, ether or ākāśa, time, space or direction, self, and mind as substances. The first four have atomic forms; composite bodies arise through combinations. Ākāśa is associated with sound, while time and direction help account for temporal and spatial judgments. Self is the bearer of cognition, desire, pleasure, pain, merit, and demerit; mind is an atomic internal organ required to explain why one embodied self does not attend to all sensory contacts simultaneously. This is pluralist realism, not reductive materialism.',
    'English labels can import the wrong picture. “Ether” is not the discarded luminiferous ether of nineteenth-century physics, “mind” is not identical with consciousness, and “substance” does not mean chemical substance. Likewise, ātman in this system is not automatically the nondual self of Advaita Vedānta. The categories belong to an argumentative ecology in which enduring selves, imperceptible entities, moral causation, and liberation coexist with atomism. Calling Kaṇāda a materialist or a physicist ahead of his time selects one strand and erases most of the ontology.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('atomism','Atoms, compounds, and wholes',[
    'Vaiśeṣika atoms are eternal, indivisible, imperceptible units associated with earth, water, fire, and air. Composite things are produced and destroyed as conjunctions and structures change. Later accounts discuss dyads, larger aggregates, heating, and the sensible qualities that emerge in compounds. The theory is speculative and inferential: atoms explain why division cannot regress forever, why material products change while basic elements endure, and how perceptible wholes arise from imperceptible components. It is not an experimental atomic theory with modern elements, forces, equations, or laboratory confirmation.',
    'Wholes introduce a distinctive commitment. A pot is not merely a heap of atoms or parts; it is a new whole inhering in its constituents. This helps explain why we perceive and act on one pot rather than an unstructured multiplicity, but it creates questions about how an additional entity relates to parts. Buddhist critics reject enduring wholes and atoms in different ways, Jain atomists develop another account of matter and combination, and Mīmāṃsā or Vedānta authors challenge Vaiśeṣika assumptions. “Indian atomism” is therefore a debate among rival theories, not one national doctrine.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-science'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('universals','Universals, particularizers, and inherence',[
    'If several cows are cows and several earth atoms belong to one kind, Vaiśeṣika asks what grounds sameness without erasing numerical difference. Universals are repeatable entities inhering in many individuals. At the other extreme, viśeṣa, ultimate particularity or differentiator, distinguishes eternal entities that would otherwise share all qualitative features. The school’s name is traditionally linked to this concern for distinctive reality. Later interpreters disagree about the scope and hierarchy of universals, and Buddhist nominalists attack the explanatory need for real repeatables.',
    'Samavāya, inherence, is introduced as an irreducible relation for connections that cannot be separated while their terms remain what they are: substance and quality, universal and instance, whole and parts, or motion and mover. The relation prevents every attribution from being reduced to loose conjunction. Critics charge that relating inherence to its relata invites regress or obscurity; Naiyāyikas and Vaiśeṣikas develop replies. The concept matters because the category system is not only an inventory of entities. It is a theory of how structured things hold together and how true predication answers to that structure.',
  ],['metaphysics','logic'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('causation','Production, absence, motion, and unseen conditions',[
    'Vaiśeṣika commonly treats an effect as a new production rather than something already manifest in its material cause. Clay is an appropriate cause for a pot, yet pothood and the whole pot begin when causal conditions operate. Later Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika distinguishes inherent or material, non-inherent, and efficient causes, and analyzes prior absence as removed when an effect appears. These distinctions sharpen arguments with Sāṃkhya theories that the effect somehow pre-exists in its cause. They also show why later absence theory cannot simply be inserted into every early aphorism.',
    'Motion includes upward, downward, contracting, expanding, and locomotor patterns in received classifications. Weight, fluidity, effort, impact, and residual disposition explain some changes; adṛṣṭa, the unseen, helps later authors connect otherwise unexplained motion, cosmic ordering, and karmic results. Theistic Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika accounts eventually assign God an important causal role, but the early sūtra’s theology and the history of this development are disputed. It is safer to distinguish early dharma and unseen causation from later systematic proofs of a divine maker than to label the entire tradition either atheistic physics or creationism.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-science'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('knowledge','Perception, inference, and knowing imperceptibles',[
    'The Vaiśeṣika tradition is often described as accepting perception and inference as basic means of knowledge, while later combined Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika works incorporate the fuller Nyāya scheme and analyze testimony and comparison. That historical qualification matters. Atom, self, mind, universal, and inherence are not all ordinary sensory objects; arguments infer them because they are claimed to explain observed qualities, temporal sequences, language, or cognition. The system’s realism is therefore not naïve reliance on vision. It joins common experience with disciplined inference to entities posited as its necessary grounds.',
    'Cognition itself is usually classified as a quality of self, produced when self, mind, sense faculty, and object stand in appropriate relations. This differs from identifying the self with an uninterrupted flow of awareness. Memory, doubt, error, pleasure, desire, and effort receive causal analysis, especially in later texts. Buddhist and Mīmāṃsā opponents contest the enduring knower, the status of universals, and the reliability or self-presentation of cognition. A Kaṇāda profile should route readers into these debates without crediting every mature Nyāya epistemological formula to the early Vaiśeṣika Sūtra.',
  ],['epistemology','philosophy-of-mind','logic'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('dharma','Classification in the service of liberation',[
    'The sūtra begins with dharma and presents knowledge of categories as connected with the highest good. Vaiśeṣika is therefore not disinterested proto-science. True discrimination among self, body, qualities, actions, and causal conditions contributes to freedom from suffering and rebirth. Merit and demerit help explain embodied experience, while attachment and action sustain bondage. Later sources describe liberation as a condition in which the self is free from pain and cognition-producing contacts, a conclusion that has generated debate over whether liberation is positive fulfillment or mere absence of suffering.',
    'This soteriological setting changes how classification should be taught. Distinguishing self from body is not only a metaphysical puzzle; it is supposed to transform attachment. Understanding causal and moral order shapes action, ritual duty, and the trajectory of rebirth. Yet the path is less fully elaborated in the surviving sūtras than the categorical system, and later Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika, devotional, and commentarial accounts supply different emphases. A concise beginner explanation should therefore say that accurate classification serves liberation, not pretend the record yields one detailed practice manual personally authored by Kaṇāda.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('reception','Praśastapāda and the Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika inheritance',[
    'Praśastapāda’s Padārthadharmasaṃgraha, probably from the first millennium CE, reorganizes and expands Vaiśeṣika into a systematic exposition rather than a line-by-line sūtra commentary. Many textbook descriptions of atoms, qualities, creation, cognition, and category relations reach readers through this later synthesis. Subsequent commentators such as Vyomaśiva, Śrīdhara, and Udayana develop it further. When a claim appears clearly only in that stream, it should be credited to Praśastapāda or later Vaiśeṣika, not retroactively turned into Kaṇāda’s speech.',
    'Vaiśeṣika and Nyāya gradually become a combined intellectual tradition. Vaiśeṣika supplies a powerful ontology and Nyāya a more extensive epistemology, logic, and debate method, though the merger is neither instantaneous nor complete. Later authors refine absence, language, God, universals, causation, and inference while engaging Buddhist, Jain, Mīmāṃsā, Vedānta, and grammarian opponents. The school’s historical achievement is thus cumulative. Kaṇāda functions as a canonical origin figure for an evolving textual lineage, just as a river’s named source does not contain every structure built downstream.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','logic','metaphysics'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra','Padārthadharmasaṃgraha']),
  section('comparison','Comparison without “ancient modern science”',[
    'Comparisons with Democritus, Aristotle, contemporary metaphysics, or particle physics can clarify questions about simples, composition, universals, qualities, and causation. They become misleading when resemblance is treated as identity or priority competition. Vaiśeṣika atoms have element-specific qualities, combine within karmic and cosmological frameworks, and coexist with selves, mind, universals, time, direction, and inherence. No evidence makes Kaṇāda the discoverer of modern atomic structure. Calling the system “scientific” is defensible only if one specifies an inferential natural-philosophical project rather than importing experimental institutions and equations.',
    'A more fruitful comparison asks what explanatory costs follow from positing wholes in addition to parts, universals in addition to instances, or inherence in addition to ordinary relations. Contemporary readers can test whether objects are reducible to components, how identical particles are individuated, and whether absence is something known or something real. The answers need not vindicate Vaiśeṣika. Its value lies in a rigorously different ontology that makes familiar assumptions visible, while its limitations and metaphysical commitments remain available for criticism rather than being protected by civilizational celebration.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-science'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra']),
  section('reading','Reading from sūtra to system and criticism',[
    'Begin with Vaiśeṣika Sūtra 1.1 in Sinha’s translation to see dharma, highest good, and the initial enumeration. Continue selectively through passages on substances, qualities, motion, conjunction, cognition, self, and liberation, keeping the Upaskāra commentary visually distinct from the root aphorisms. Use Potter’s historical summaries to identify alternative recensions, dates, and later developments. Maintain three columns in notes: received sūtra, commentator’s explanation, and modern reconstruction. That simple habit prevents the most common attribution error in reading an aphoristic tradition.',
    'Then study Praśastapāda and the category discussions mapped by Ganeri, asking which six categories are early and when absence becomes a recognized seventh. Use Chatterjee to compare Nyāya–Vaiśeṣika atoms with Jain and Buddhist theories, especially wholes, differentiators, and causation. Finally read objections to universals and inherence rather than presenting the system only from inside. A good beginner finishes able to explain not only that Vaiśeṣika has atoms and categories, but why it posits each kind of entity, which claims belong to later authors, and where opponents locate explanatory excess.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','metaphysics','logic'],[],['Vaiśeṣika Sūtra','Padārthadharmasaṃgraha']),
];

const patanjaliSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence','Patañjali as an authorship and textual-history problem',[
    'Patañjali is the name attached to the Yoga Sūtra tradition, but there is no secure biography of its author or redactor. Later tradition sometimes identifies this Patañjali with the author of the Mahābhāṣya on grammar and with a medical authority. The identification is first clearly attested long after the relevant works and remains disputed; shared name and retrospective praise do not establish one polymath. This page therefore treats the Yoga Patañjali as a textual attribution, not as a documented person whose grammatical, medical, and yogic careers can be combined into one life story.',
    'Dating is likewise unsettled. Older scholarship often placed the Yoga Sūtras in the early centuries CE and some proposals range earlier, while philological work on the integrated Pātañjalayogaśāstra argues for approximately the fourth century CE. The text must predate secure later commentaries, but relative references and philosophical parallels do not produce an exact year. The corrected date label is a floruit or composition horizon—“uncertain; Pātañjalayogaśāstra often placed around the fourth century CE”—rather than the misleading literal lifespan from 200 BCE to 200 CE found in older Atlas data.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Yoga Sūtra','Yogabhāṣya']),
  section('text','Sūtra, bhāṣya, and a possible unified work',[
    'The received Yoga Sūtras comprise four chapters or pādas traditionally read with the earliest surviving commentary, the Yogabhāṣya attributed to Vyāsa. Because individual sūtras are extremely compressed, commentary supplies definitions, examples, objections, and doctrinal connections needed to understand them. Traditional study ordinarily approaches the pair together. Edwin Bryant and many earlier readers preserve the distinction between Patañjali’s aphorisms and Vyāsa’s commentary, while Philipp Maas’s manuscript and textual research argues that both formed one authorial Pātañjalayogaśāstra, a treatise in sūtra-and-exposition form.',
    'The unified-work hypothesis is influential, not an excuse to erase all debate or rename every familiar route silently. It changes attribution practice: a detailed doctrine stated only in the bhāṣya may belong to the same composition under one model, but to a foundational commentator under another. This page names the layer whenever it matters and avoids pretending that “Patañjali says” is always straightforward. Vācaspati Miśra, Bhoja, Vijñānabhikṣu, the disputed Vivaraṇa, and other later commentators further shape what readers call classical Yoga. Their agreements and disagreements are reception evidence, not direct access to an unmediated founder.',
  ],['indian-philosophy'],[],['Yoga Sūtra','Yogabhāṣya','Pātañjalayogaśāstra']),
  section('before','Yoga before and beyond Patañjali',[
    'The Yoga Sūtra does not invent yoga. Vedic ascetic materials, early Upaniṣads, the Mahābhārata and Bhagavad Gītā, Buddhist and Jain traditions, and multiple renunciant communities preserve disciplines of posture, breath, sense restraint, concentration, meditation, ethical restraint, and liberation. These traditions share vocabulary and practices while disagreeing about self, causation, knowledge, and final freedom. Claims of direct borrowing require particular evidence; a common ascetic environment is safer than a simple story in which one religion owns meditation and the others copy it.',
    'The opening “now, instruction in yoga” presents a teaching already available for disciplined transmission. Pātañjala Yoga becomes the canonical philosophical system associated with this text, but yoga remains a wider family of paths and practices. Six-limbed, eight-limbed, devotional, knowledge-centered, tantric, haṭha, and modern postural yogas cannot be reduced to variants of one original Patañjali program. Conversely, the global popularity of the word yoga does not make the Yoga Sūtra a generic manual for anything called yogic. Its distinctive dualism, psychology, ethics, meditative sequence, and liberation goal need to remain visible.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','buddhist-philosophy','jainism'],['buddha','mahavira'],['Yoga Sūtra','Bhagavad Gītā']),
  section('definition','Stilling the activities of citta',[
    'Yoga Sūtra 1.2 defines yoga through citta-vṛtti-nirodha. Translations include cessation, restriction, stilling, or control of the fluctuations, activities, or turnings of mind. Each choice interprets technical terms. Citta is the material-psychological apparatus that presents experience to consciousness; it is not simply brain, soul, or everyday “mind.” Vṛtti includes cognition and mental activity, even reliable cognition, while nirodha denotes the disciplined quieting central to the path. The line should therefore be cited to a named translation, not circulated as a transparent inspirational quotation.',
    'When the activities are stilled, the seer is said to abide in its own form; otherwise it identifies with those activities. The “seer” is puruṣa, consciousness distinct from prakṛti, material nature and its transformations. Suffering arises because consciousness appears entangled with a mind-body complex it illuminates but is not. Practice does not manufacture puruṣa or merge it with a cosmic substance. It clarifies discriminative awareness so that identification ceases. This framework differs from Buddhist no-self analysis, Advaita nondualism, and many modern psychological readings, even when useful comparisons can be made.',
  ],['philosophy-of-mind','metaphysics'],[],['Yoga Sūtra']),
  section('samkhya','A Sāṃkhya-related metaphysics with distinctive commitments',[
    'Pātañjala Yoga shares with classical Sāṃkhya a plurality of puruṣas and an evolving prakṛti structured by three guṇas—sattva, rajas, and tamas. Intellect, ego function, mind, senses, subtle elements, and gross elements belong to prakṛti. Consciousness does not itself act, yet experience occurs when the subtle mental apparatus takes forms and is illuminated as if conscious. Liberation requires discriminating the seer from the seen. Calling this “mind over matter” is wrong because mind is itself on the material side of the distinction.',
    'Yoga is not simply Sāṃkhya with exercise. It foregrounds meditative discipline and admits Īśvara, a special puruṣa untouched by affliction, action, karmic result, and latent deposit. Scholars and commentators disagree about how central this commitment is and whether “God” is the best English label. Īśvara is not straightforwardly the creator God of later theologies within the sūtras, though later interpreters may integrate Pātañjala practice into devotional systems. The relation between Sāṃkhya and Yoga also changes historically, so two eternally separate textbook schools should not be projected into every early source.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Yoga Sūtra','Yogabhāṣya']),
  section('afflictions','Affliction, karma, and the construction of suffering',[
    'The five kleśas are ignorance, I-making or egoity, attachment, aversion, and clinging to life. Ignorance is not missing miscellaneous information; it is a deep confusion that treats the impermanent as permanent, the impure as pure, suffering as pleasure, and nonself as self. From it arise misidentification and reactive habits. Actions leave karmic deposits and latent tendencies that mature in future experience, embodiment, and lifespan. Yoga addresses this causal depth rather than promising relaxation alone. Pleasant and painful experiences can both reinforce bondage when appropriated through ignorance.',
    'Kriyā-yoga—discipline or austerity, study or recitation, and dedication to Īśvara—attenuates afflictions and prepares concentration. The path combines analysis with repeated transformation of habit. A beginner can compare an automatic reaction that becomes visible and loses force through attention, but the classical claim extends across rebirth and a metaphysical account of consciousness. Modern therapeutic analogy must therefore be labeled analogy. Yoga’s diagnosis is also not that embodiment itself is a moral failure; bondage lies in ignorance and identification within a beginningless relation whose workings the text seeks to reverse.',
  ],['philosophy-of-mind','ethics'],[],['Yoga Sūtra']),
  section('eight-limbs','Eight limbs as an integrated discipline',[
    'The eight limbs are ethical restraints, observances, posture, regulation of breath, withdrawal of the senses, concentration, meditation, and samādhi. They move from conduct and embodied preparation toward increasingly stable attention, but readers debate how strictly sequential they are. The five restraints—nonviolence, truthfulness, nonstealing, celibacy or responsible sexual restraint, and nonpossession—are called a great vow when applied without limitation by status, place, time, or circumstance. The observances include purity, contentment, disciplined effort, study, and dedication to Īśvara. Ethics is therefore constitutive of concentration, not optional lifestyle branding.',
    'The Yoga Sūtra gives posture only a few aphorisms, describing stability and ease and release of effort. Later commentaries name postures, and later haṭha and modern yoga traditions greatly expand bodily practice. This historical change does not make modern postural yoga fake, but it prevents the claim that today’s studio sequence is simply Patañjali’s third limb unchanged. Breath and sense disciplines reduce disturbance; concentration fixes attention; meditation sustains its flow; samādhi deepens absorption. Each term has a commentarial history that a one-word English list cannot communicate.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Yoga Sūtra']),
  section('knowledge','Reliable cognition and extraordinary knowing',[
    'The text classifies mental activities including reliable cognition, error, conceptual construction, sleep, and memory. Perception, inference, and authoritative testimony are accepted as means of reliable cognition within ordinary inquiry. Yet even accurate cognition is still a vṛtti to be stilled in the final path; practical truth is not identical with liberation. This distinction prevents two errors: Yoga is not hostile to reasoning, and successful reasoning alone does not isolate consciousness. Discriminative knowledge becomes liberating through sustained cultivation that transforms how experience is appropriated.',
    'Later chapters describe concentrated integrations, saṃyama, and extraordinary capacities associated with them: knowledge of minds or past lives, unusual bodily powers, and other siddhis. The text also warns that such attainments can obstruct the highest samādhi. Modern readers may interpret these claims devotionally, psychologically, historically, or skeptically, but an editorial page should neither quietly delete them nor certify them as demonstrated science. Their presence shows that classical Yoga’s epistemic world exceeds secular mindfulness. The careful question is what role extraordinary cognition plays within the text’s hierarchy of goals and warnings.',
  ],['epistemology','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Yoga Sūtra']),
  section('isvara','Īśvara, oṃ, and the limits of the “atheistic yoga” label',[
    'Īśvarapraṇidhāna appears both as a route to samādhi and as a practice within kriyā-yoga and the observances. Īśvara is a special puruṣa beyond affliction and karmic conditioning, an unsurpassed seed of omniscience and teacher not limited by time. The syllable oṃ designates Īśvara; repetition and contemplation are prescribed. This is genuine devotional and contemplative material. Describing Patañjali’s Yoga as simply atheistic erases it, while calling Īśvara a creator identical to every later Hindu theology adds claims the sūtras do not clearly make.',
    'Commentators debate whether dedication is optional, central, or the most accessible path; later Vedānta and devotional interpreters assimilate the figure differently. The philosophical tension is productive. If each puruṣa is isolated consciousness and prakṛti evolves through its own causal order, what explanatory and practical work does a special puruṣa perform? The text emphasizes an ideal object and timeless teacher more than cosmic creation. Translation should sometimes retain Īśvara precisely because “Lord” and “God” can imply doctrines absent from the immediate argument. The term’s function must be explained, not settled by one English noun.',
  ],['philosophy-of-religion','metaphysics'],[],['Yoga Sūtra']),
  section('liberation','Discriminative discernment and kaivalya',[
    'The goal is kaivalya, isolation or aloneness: puruṣa no longer identifies with prakṛti’s cognitive and affective processes, and the guṇas cease serving that puruṣa’s experience and liberation. Translation can make this sound lonely, solipsistic, or dissociative. In the system it names freedom from misidentification and karmic suffering, not social withdrawal as an everyday mood. Because there are many puruṣas, liberation does not absorb the individual into one consciousness. Nor is it merely a temporary altered state; it is an irreversible soteriological achievement.',
    'Scholars disagree about whether classical Yoga’s dualism devalues embodied life or instead provides an integrated discipline within it. Ethical vows, careful cognition, bodily stability, breath, study, devotion, and attention all use prakṛti to undo confusion. The mind can become so clear that it reflects the distinction between consciousness and nature, but it does not turn into puruṣa. Modern nondual and embodied yoga traditions may reinterpret this end. They belong to reception rather than serving as neutral translations of kaivalya. Comparison should preserve the possibility that later yogas intentionally reject or transform Pātañjala metaphysics.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Yoga Sūtra','Yogabhāṣya']),
  section('reception','Commentaries, eclipse, revival, and global yoga',[
    'Vācaspati Miśra’s Tattvavaiśāradī, Bhoja’s Rājamārtaṇḍa, Vijñānabhikṣu’s Yogavārttika, and other commentaries transmit and adapt Pātañjala Yoga. Al-Bīrūnī’s Arabic rendering demonstrates medieval cross-cultural reception. The text’s later prestige did not mean it always dominated every practice called yoga; tantric and haṭha traditions developed different bodies, techniques, and goals. Modern reformers, translators, Theosophists, nationalists, teachers, and global posture movements elevated “Patañjali” as an ancient authority in new institutional and political settings. Canonical status is therefore historically renewed, not simply constant.',
    'Contemporary yoga uses the sūtras for meditation, ethics, psychology, spirituality, teacher training, wellness, and identity. Such uses can be creative without being ancient originals. Problems arise when modern posture is projected backward, Hindu location is erased, one commentator is marketed as the only authentic voice, or biomedical language recasts liberation claims as laboratory findings without evidence. Responsible reception names its reconstruction. A practitioner may draw on nonviolence or attention while disagreeing with dualism; a historian may study modern transformation without deciding the text’s spiritual truth. Both should identify which Patañjali they are invoking.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','ethics'],[],['Yoga Sūtra','Yogabhāṣya']),
  section('reading','Reading a terse text through explicit lenses',[
    'Begin with a named translation that prints standard chapter and sūtra numbers and distinguishes root text from commentary. Read 1.1–1.16 for definition, activities, practice, and dispassion; 1.23–1.29 for Īśvara and oṃ; 2.1–2.29 for afflictions, action, and the path; 2.46–3.8 for posture through samādhi; and selected fourth-chapter passages on mind and liberation. Keep Bryant’s traditional-commentarial orientation visible rather than treating his English wording as the sūtra itself.',
    'Then read the IEP overview and compare Maas’s unified Pātañjalayogaśāstra hypothesis with the traditional Patañjali–Vyāsa division. Use Larson and Bhattacharya to place the work within Sāṃkhya-Yoga and a larger commentarial corpus, and Newcombe to follow yoga beyond classical codification. Track five translation problems—citta, vṛtti, nirodha, puruṣa, and kaivalya—across two versions. Finally compare one Buddhist or Jain meditation account without forcing dependence or equivalence. The aim is to understand a layered philosophical tradition, not extract universal wellness slogans from 195 compressed aphorisms.',
  ],['indian-philosophy','philosophy-of-mind','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Yoga Sūtra','Yogabhāṣya','Pātañjalayogaśāstra']),
];

